+## How it works
+
+```
+ browsers (newcomer, employer, landlord, bank)
+        |   POST /api/actions          ^  SSE /api/events (full snapshots)
+        v                              |
+ Next.js app ── one in-memory store: pure reducers, a revision per change
+        |
+        |  agent (demo mode: cached and deterministic; live mode: Vertex AI, optional)
+        |        every call that would send labelled data asks first:
+        v
+ Rasikh Guard  ──  policy matrix: 9 data labels x 7 destinations, fail-closed
+        |
+        v
+ TAMM MCP server (mock catalogue)   ·   simulated UAE PASS
 ```
 
-The app is at http://127.0.0.1:3000.
+- **Shared record.** `apps/web/src/domain` holds the model (hires, steps, documents, approvals, applications, decisions, consents, Guard checks, companies), the roadmap logic and the reducers. Everything is a pure function from state and action to state, which is what makes the live sync and the reset trivial to reason about.
+- **The agent.** It explains every output in short plain sentences. In the default demo mode its responses are cached and deterministic, so the demo never depends on a network. Live mode, when enabled, uses Gemini on Vertex AI for document extraction and for explaining the next step; it is authorised by Guard first and sends only what the policy allows (documents for extraction only).
+- **Contract.** The three parts (app, Guard, TAMM MCP) talk through a written contract, [INTEGRATION.md](INTEGRATION.md), with shared types in `packages/shared`.
 
-## Environment
+## Privacy and security
 
-Set in `apps/web/.env.local`. See also INTEGRATION.md section 7.
+Rasikh Guard is a fork of [OpenAPPA](https://github.com/archestra-ai/OpenAPPA) (MIT), and in spirit it works like a policy engine such as Open Policy Agent: the policy is data, one component decides, and the decision is logged with a reason.
 
-| Variable           | Purpose                                                              | Default                     |
-| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
-| `OPENAI_API_KEY`   | Live AI mode. Server-side only, never prefixed with `NEXT_PUBLIC_`.  | none                        |
-| `RASIKH_AI_MODE`   | `demo` for cached deterministic responses, `live` for the OpenAI API | `demo`                      |
-| `RASIKH_GUARD_URL` | Rasikh Guard sidecar                                                 | `http://localhost:8787`     |
-| `TAMM_MCP_URL`     | TAMM MCP server                                                      | `http://localhost:8790/mcp` |
-| `RASIKH_DEMO_MODE` | `1` enables the dev reset and advance endpoints                      | `0`                         |
+- **Labels follow data.** Anything the agent reads is labelled, and the labels travel with it into later calls, so data cannot be laundered by re-declaring it.
+- **Per label, per destination.** Passport and Emirates ID need consent before a landlord or bank sees them; salary reaches a landlord only as a derived yes or no; bank statements never go to TAMM; health data goes only to insurance services; a degree never goes to a landlord.
+- **Consent is narrow.** One label, one destination, revocable, and it cannot unlock a cell the policy denies.
+- **Fail-closed.** If Guard is unreachable, the action does not happen.
+- **Visible.** The trust passport shows the person what they have allowed and lets them revoke it. The Guard log shows every check with its reason.
 
-## Demo mode
+**What the evidence shows, honestly.** An earlier archived evaluation failed: it allowed 12 of 25 forbidden synthetic flows. After hardening, the deployed Guard release (contract 1.2.0) was run on 2 October 2026 against the same 25 synthetic attack cases, three times, over HTTP: 75 of 75 attempts were denied, with no forbidden allows, and 99 contract-conformance checks passed with none failing (3 skipped). This is a measurement of the policy decision only. We did **not** measure model obedience, end-to-end exfiltration, the security of the whole app or production prompt parity, so we do not claim the agent is proven safe. The Guard page in the app shows the evidence and its limits, and the archived failure stays on record.
 
-Demo mode runs the same flows with cached, realistic responses, so the demo never depends on network or latency. It is the default (`RASIKH_AI_MODE=demo`) and needs no API key. The in-app toggle switches between demo and live. The demo script is in [DEMO.md](DEMO.md).
